            <ArticleTitle>Trigonal cavernous angiomas: report of three cases and review of literature.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>367-71, discussion 371</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="BACKGROUND" NlmCategory="BACKGROUND">Intraventricular cavernous angiomas are very rare. Only few cases of trigonal angiomas have been reported.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CASE DESCRIPTION" NlmCategory="METHODS">We report three cases of trigonal cavernous angiomas who presented with raised intracranial pressure or seizures and who underwent total excision with a good recovery. We also review the literature and discuss surgical approaches.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSION" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">On magnetic resonance imaging, intraventricular cavernous angiomas lack the hemosiderin ring characteristically seen around parenchymal cavernous angiomas. This explains why trigonal cavernous angiomas can mimic malignant neoplasm on imaging, and they should be considered in the differential diagnosis of intraventricular masses. Total excision should be the goal of surgery.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
